For all $\mu, \nu \in \mathcal P_2(Q)$, $y\in Q$ and $0<\varepsilon<1$  we have
\begin{eqnarray*}
 d_{\mathrm{TV}}(\mu_{\varepsilon,y},\mu)\le\varepsilon\,,&& W^2_2(\mu_{\varepsilon,y},\mu)\le \varepsilon\, F_\mu(y)\,,
\end{eqnarray*}
where the second estimate uses the coupling that leaves $(1-\varepsilon)\mu$ fixed and transports the remaining mass to $y$. In conjunction with Theorem \ref{thm:contamination} this yields that $\mathcal N$ is dense in both metrics, and with Theorem \ref{thm:pre-Ucal-dense} that $\mathcal U$ is dense in both metrics.

Moreover, if $x,y\in B(\mu)$ are distinct, the measures $\mu_{\varepsilon,x}$ and $\mu_{\varepsilon,y}$ approach $\mu$ in either metric and have unique means $x$ and $y$. Thus, a selection on any neighborhood of $\mu$ cannot be continuous there in either metric.

